window.__labLoaded({"id":"8.3","title":"R-Squared and Residuals: Is the Line Any Good?","goal":"Measure how good the study-hours line is with R squared and adjusted R squared, and use the residuals to check the fit, for the lesson's eight students.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Fit the model with statsmodels","text":"We fit the same line as in Lesson 8.2, but with statsmodels' formula interface, which gives the full summary. The formula 'score ~ hours' reads as 'score explained by hours'.","code":"import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport statsmodels.formula.api as smf\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame({'hours': [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8],\n                   'score': [48, 62, 55, 66, 63, 74, 70, 82]})\n\nmodel = smf.ols('score ~ hours', data=df).fit()\n\nprint(model.params.round(3))","out":"","figs":[]},{"title":"R squared and adjusted R squared","text":"R squared is the share of the score variation that the line explains (0 to 1). Adjusted R squared is slightly lower: it charges a small penalty for each predictor, so it is fairer when comparing models.","code":"print(f'R squared          = {model.rsquared:.3f}')\nprint(f'adjusted R squared = {model.rsquared_adj:.3f}')\nprint(f'residual std error = {np.sqrt(model.scale):.2f} points')\n\nsse = (model.resid ** 2).sum()\nprint(f'SSE = {sse:.1f}')","out":"","figs":[]},{"title":"Look at the residuals","text":"A residual is what the line missed: actual minus predicted. Residuals should add up to about zero, and no student should be missed by a wildly larger amount than the rest.","code":"df['fitted'] = model.fittedvalues\ndf['residual'] = model.resid\n\nprint(df.round(2).to_string(index=False))\nprint(f'sum of residuals = {df.residual.sum():.6f}')","out":"","figs":[]},{"title":"Plot residuals against fitted values","text":"If a straight line suits the data, the points scatter randomly around zero with no curve and no funnel shape. A visible pattern would warn us that the model is missing something.","code":"plt.figure(figsize=(6, 3.4))\nplt.scatter(df.fitted, df.residual, color='#1f77b4')\nplt.axhline(0, color='#d62728', linestyle='--')\nplt.xlabel('Fitted score')\nplt.ylabel('Residual')\nplt.title('Residuals versus fitted values')\nplt.show()","out":"","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"hours <- c(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8)\nscore <- c(48, 62, 55, 66, 63, 74, 70, 82)\nfit <- lm(score ~ hours)\nsummary(fit)\nround(residuals(fit), 2)\nplot(fitted(fit), residuals(fit)); abline(h = 0)","expect":"Multiple R-squared: 0.8225, Adjusted R-squared: 0.7929; Residual standard error: 4.863 on 6 degrees of freedom; residuals -3.17 6.88 -4.07 2.98 -3.98 3.07 -4.88 3.17","note":"summary(lm) shows both R-squared values and the residual standard error together."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Regression > Linear (Save: Residuals and Predicted values; Plots: *ZRESID on Y, *ZPRED on X)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / hours score.\nBEGIN DATA\n1 48  2 62  3 55  4 66  5 63  6 74  7 70  8 82\nEND DATA.\nREGRESSION\n  /STATISTICS COEFF R ANOVA\n  /DEPENDENT score\n  /METHOD=ENTER hours\n  /SCATTERPLOT=(*ZRESID ,*ZPRED)\n  /SAVE PRED RESID.","note":"The Model Summary table has R Square, Adjusted R Square and Std. Error of the Estimate. SAVE adds PRE_1 and RES_1 columns to the data. The plot uses standardized residuals, so its axis units differ from Python's, but the shape is the same."},"stata":{"code":"clear\nset obs 8\ngenerate hours = _n\ngenerate score = real(word(\"48 62 55 66 63 74 70 82\", _n))\nregress score hours\npredict fitted, xb\npredict resid, residuals\nlist hours score fitted resid\nrvfplot, yline(0)","note":"regress prints R-squared, Adj R-squared and Root MSE (the residual standard error). predict creates the fitted values and residuals; rvfplot draws residuals versus fitted."},"try":["Change the last score from 82 to 60. Predict what happens to R squared and to the biggest residual, then run it.","Look at the residual plot. Which student is missed most, and in which direction?"]});
